 ## Overview
-This method defines the mechanical geometry and dynamic balancing strategy for a rotating double-helix reflector used in laser-based volumetric displays [S1, unofficial]. It solves the problem of maintaining structural integrity and optical stability at high rotational speeds by utilizing axisymmetric contours to balance centrifugal forces and continuous curvature to maximize bending stiffness [S1, unofficial]. It is distinguished from standard cylindrical or planar rotating mirrors by its specific double-helix topology, which minimizes aerodynamic drag and vibration while enabling "walk-around" viewing of the projected 3D volume [S1, unofficial].
+This method defines the mechanical geometry and dynamic balancing strategy for a rotating double-helix reflector used in laser-based volumetric displays [S1, unofficial]. It solves the problem of maintaining structural integrity and optical stability at high rotational speeds by utilizing axisymmetric contours to balance centrifugal forces and continuous curvature to maximize bending stiffness [S1, unofficial]. It builds on [Volumetric Display using Rotating Mirror (VRDM)](2567-volumetric-display-using-rotating-mirror-vrdm.md) and is distinguished from standard cylindrical or planar rotating mirrors by its specific double-helix topology, which minimizes aerodynamic drag and vibration while enabling "walk-around" viewing of the projected 3D volume [S1, unofficial].
 
